# Local development only. Copy to `.env` beside this file and fill in what you
# need — `.env` is gitignored, this file is not, so never put a real secret here.
#
#   cp apps/web/.env.example apps/web/.env
#
# Self-hosting with Docker does NOT use this file: set these in the
# `environment:` block of docker-compose.yml instead, where they are already
# listed with the same comments. A real environment variable always wins over
# this file, so the two can never fight.
#
# Everything here is optional. With none of it set the app runs on local disk
# in ./data and needs no configuration at all.

# Public origin of this instance, used to build published-board links. Unset is
# fine locally — the origin is read off each request instead.
# SUBSTRATUM_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Where the SQLite database and, on local disk, the images live.
# SUBSTRATUM_DATA_DIR=./data


# --- S3-compatible object storage (optional) ---------------------------------
#
# Setting SUBSTRATUM_S3_BUCKET is what moves image storage off local disk. The
# first four go together: set the bucket and leave one of the others out and the
# app stops at startup naming what's missing, rather than quietly carrying on
# against local disk and splitting your library across two places.
#
# Copy any images you already have across BEFORE turning this on, or every image
# saved until now shows as broken while sitting untouched on disk:
#
#   pnpm migrate:storage
#
# Note that a bucket makes the data volume no longer a complete backup — the
# database stays on disk, the images live in the bucket, and you need both. See
# the README.

# SUBSTRATUM_S3_BUCKET=substratum
# SUBSTRATUM_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# SUBSTRATUM_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# The API origin, including the scheme. A bare host or host:port is rejected at
# startup, since it cannot be told apart from a URL with a strange scheme.
#
#   Cloudflare R2   https://<account-id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
#   Backblaze B2    https://s3.<region>.backblazeb2.com
#   MinIO, local    http://localhost:9000
#   AWS S3          https://s3.<region>.amazonaws.com
# SUBSTRATUM_S3_ENDPOINT=

# R2 wants "auto". MinIO and most others are content with the default of
# us-east-1. The region goes into the request signature, so a wrong one comes
# back as SignatureDoesNotMatch — which reads exactly like a bad secret key.
# SUBSTRATUM_S3_REGION=auto

# Path-style addressing (/bucket/key), which R2, B2 and MinIO all accept and
# which is the default. Set false for an AWS S3 bucket created after 2020.
# SUBSTRATUM_S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=true

# Key prefix, so one bucket can hold more than one instance.
# SUBSTRATUM_S3_PREFIX=
